WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
SPEAKER 1: What was in the
mind of Winston Churchill?

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.300
Why did he think that he
could afford not to negotiate?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.695
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.100
In terms of supreme command,
the Allies were very lucky.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:26.370
Because had the war broken out
when Stanley Baldwin was Prime

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.500
Minister, I think Britain would
have not done very well at all.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.390
It broke out when Neville
Chamberlain was prime minister.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.730
But quickly, he
began to bring people

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.900
in the cabinet who advised
him, corrected him,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.930
educated him about
what was going on.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.700
He still felt that there was
some chance of a negotiated

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.010
peace after the fall of France.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:55.540
But on May 10, 1940, the day
that Germany invaded France,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.450
there was a radical
change of government.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.140
Winston Churchill
came in, and Churchill

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.769
was determined to not
surrender, not negotiate,

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:08.520
but not only fight the
Germans and the Italians,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.750
but defeat them.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.850
Now, this was a
very strange idea

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:17.140
because, within three weeks,
there would just be Britain,

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.230
and there would be all
of occupied Europe.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.690
So what was in the mind
of Winston Churchill?

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.920
Why did he think that he
could afford not to negotiate?

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.330
Hitler had offered him
a deal where he said,

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:31.200
we will not invade
or bomb you, and we

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.600
will let you have your empire.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.400
Just call off the war.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.100
And then Hitler thought that he
would turn his attention then

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.920
without a worry over either
Britain or the United States,

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.190
and he could turn his
attention to the Soviet Union.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.350
But Churchill was
not just a romantic.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.300
He was a very brilliant
guy who was convinced

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.760
that the last minute armament
of the Royal Air Force,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.760
and given the fact that the
Royal Navy was the largest

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.150
navy in the world, meant
everybody took a deep breath.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.950
They said to themselves, for all
of Germans' continental ground

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:12.480
power, land forces, they
cannot get across the channel

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.670
because the British
Navy will sink it,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.830
sink ships from the German Navy.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.640
And they do not have the ability
to achieve air superiority

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.240
over Britain.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.400
And, therefore, they
can't attack us.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.230
And Churchill said,
well, you know what?

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.920
We can't invade either.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.590
So there's going
to be a standoff.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:33.180
And that standoff is going to
favor us, because eventually,

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.140
authoritarians like Hitler and
Stalin cannot trust each other.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.610
They'll have a falling out.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.890
And at some point, they
will fight each other,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.470
and that will be
to our advantage.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.800
And at some point, we understand
that either the Japanese

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.650
or the Germans or the Italians
will do something stupid

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.400
and draw the United
States into the war.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.310
Churchill was already
envisioning an alliance

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.700
that was to happen a year and
a half later of the Soviet

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.290
Union, the United States,
and the British empire

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.090
against the Axis triad.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.040
And they would have
enormous advantages

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.560
in terms of
manpower, population,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.250
industrial strength,
and geography.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.050
And that's exactly
what happened.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.180
In America, it's hard to think
how we would have been prepared

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.060
for Pearl Harbor without
Franklin Roosevelt. By that, I

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.840
mean the Republican Party
was primarily isolationist.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.960
And the Democratic
Party was focused

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.510
on the Great Depression.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.340
And so, at this time,
people were saying,

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.360
we cannot invest in military
expenses and armies and navies

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.860
and air forces.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.400
FDR, along with a brilliant
congressman, Carl Vinson,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.480
thought otherwise.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.130
And FDR understood that wherever
we were going to fight, Pacific

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.380
or European theaters,
we needed a navy

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.620
to transport soldiers over
there and to protect them

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.310
from coming over here.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.500
So in a series of Naval Acts, in
the late 1930s, the Carl Vinson

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:07.620
Naval Act 1, 2, 3, and et
cetera, we had on the books

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.270
a new Pacific fleet,
about five times larger

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.760
than was at Pearl
Harbor, and much better,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.279
characterized by Baltimore
heavy cruisers, Essex carriers,

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.410
the Iowa-class battleships.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.420
That was already on the books.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.860
It was already budgeted before
we went in to World War II.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:33.000
And FDR, in sometimes cagey,
Machiavellian fashion,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.170
was reassuring an
isolationist nation in 1940

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.100
that it would not go to war,
even though he was taking

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.610
quite dramatic steps to
prepare it to go to war

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.820
and might even welcome
it going to war.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.030
And the Second World War was
ultimately a story of people,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.720
how people died, and how people
made decisions that determined

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.490
which side won or lost.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.590
I think we can sum
it up by the results,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:02.250
because the proof of the
pudding is always in the eating.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.490
And when you look at this
vast canvas of death that

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.890
was the Second World
War, 65 million people,

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.140
and we have to
ask ourselves, why

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.960
were they able to be so
successful in the war?

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.420
And I think the answer
is the United States

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.930
and the British had an
overarching principle

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.740
that they were going to
find ways to win the war

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.840
and to wage it with the
least possible injury

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.940
to their own
country's populations.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.910
And that was completely
diametrically opposed

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.010
to the Axis powers'
war of aggression

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.740
where they thought that
we're super people.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.810
We're going to
destroy inferiors,

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.390
and if we lose people,
it's a glorious thing

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.070
to die during a war.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.790
Or in the Soviet Union's
idea that all that matters is

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.410
survival of the Soviet state.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.620
Millions do not matter
if the Soviet principal,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.810
the Soviet project survives.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.940
Ultimately, then,
the story of people

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.640
turns out to be the brilliance
of Winston Churchill

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.910
and Franklin Roosevelt, and
leading the democracies, not

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.900
just to war, but
to a victorious war

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.750
and at very little
cost in comparison

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.160
to the other belligerents.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:17.720
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.780
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlight series

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:23.140
and invite you to explore our
complete course on the Second

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:24.490
World Wars.

